  "id": "pr-196",
  "instruction": "Make nested glom specifications behave consistently wherever an operator accepts a child specification. Child specs must be evaluated against the current target and scope, including when they are nested inside Spec wrappers or other composite specifications, rather than being returned as literal values. Preserve an explicit way to request literal values, and do not reinterpret structural arguments such as path strings, mapping keys, match predicates, or switch case labels as specs.\n\nThis behavior must cover recursive Spec composition, Match fallbacks, Switch selectors/cases/fallbacks, Assign values, subspecs embedded in T expressions, and values assigned through S scope variables. Match and Switch defaults must be evaluated as child specs when supplied, while omitting a default must retain their normal no-match behavior. Assign must evaluate a spec-valued assignment before storing it, and S assignments must evaluate their right-hand side in the current scope so later specifications can read the assigned variable. The same rules must hold through multiple levels of nesting and for combinations with dot-delimited paths, callable invocations, T expressions, and other existing glom features.\n\nThe completed behavior is observable through the existing tests named “spec and recursion”, “match default”, “switch”, “assign spec val”, “t subspec”, and “s scope assign”: each must pass without special-casing only one nesting depth or one operator, and existing literal-value and error behavior must remain compatible.",
